{
  "dom_id": "step-q105617",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$$\nf(\\theta ,\u03d5)=\u27e85\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\text{_____}\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,\u03d5)$ above parametrizes the sphere defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25.$ What is the third component of $f?$",
  "worked_solution": "The given equation generates a sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\nIn spherical polar coordinates, this sphere has the equation $\\rho =5.$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5)$ are the spherical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e85\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e85\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $\u03d5\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5.$",
